# The policy is hosted by the factory, not requested from the owner

Play asks every listing for a privacy-policy URL, and the factory answered badly: `2026-09-22-smoke`,
`2026-09-23-pipe-proof` and `2026-09-25-pipe-sizing` all carry `https://example.com/privacy` — a
placeholder that is not a policy — and `2026-10-02-laneset` declared none until it was published. The
owner's instruction,
recorded 2026-10-02, is that the factory stop leaving this to whoever remembers: *generate the policy
and use it — no stubs, because stubs are not needed; real, immediately.*

## Rules

- **The policy is generated from the app's real data practices.** `store/privacy.md` is written by
  `scripts/store/render_documents.py` from the pack's declarations, preserving human edits; it is the
  committed source of truth. Never hand the user a policy that does not describe the shipped app.
- **It is hosted at a real public HTTPS URL, and the factory does the hosting.** The command is
  `node scripts/publish-privacy.mjs --slot <slug>`; the runbook is `docs/privacy-policy-hosting.md`.
  Putting the question to the owner — "where will you host it?" — is the defect this rule exists to
  stop.
- **No placeholder reaches a release.** A reserved documentation host is not a final state:
  `pack.placeholders` (`scripts/core/assets.mjs`) fails a `privacyPolicyUrl` on `example.com`,
  `localhost`, a non-HTTPS scheme, or none at all.
- **The same URL is reachable from inside the app.** The in-app half is a separate Play requirement,
  measured by `slot.inAppPolicy` (`scripts/check-in-app-policy.mjs`): the URL is a
  `translatable="false"` resource opened through `ACTION_VIEW`, `CustomTabsIntent` or
  `LocalUriHandler`. Privacy prose in an About screen is not the policy.
- **The URL is a build input, so publishing happens during development**, before the app is reviewed —
  not in `/release` (`.cursor/rules/release-gate.mdc`). Rewriting the listing to publish it voids a
  recorded release approval, so publish first and re-record only on the owner's own words.

## How it is verified

```bash
node scripts/publish-privacy.mjs --slot <slug>       # publish, verify reachable, record the URL
node scripts/publish-privacy.mjs --slot <slug> --check
node scripts/check-in-app-policy.mjs --slot <slug>   # the app can open the declared policy
node scripts/verify.mjs --slot <slug>                # pack.placeholders + slot.inAppPolicy
```

The publisher refuses to publish a stub and refuses to record a URL it could not fetch — the two
guards are the `refusing to publish a stub` check and the `is not readable` check in
`scripts/publish-privacy.mjs`. They are named by the message they print rather than by line, because
these lines moved twice in one session while these sentences were being written: a citation that
drifts stops being evidence.

## The honest limit

The publisher verifies HTTP 200 and that the body mentions the policy — it cannot prove the page
matches the shipped app, that the markdown conversion kept every disclosure, or that the policy is
legally adequate. Confirming that the policy still describes the app after the app's data practices
change stays a human reading, named in the runbook § "The residual human act"; no program here can
make that judgement.
